#{[:db/ident :activity.type/assignment {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461 :db/ident :activity.type/assignment}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/free {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167 :db/ident :answer-field.type/free}] [:db/ident :answer.type/math {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math}] [:db/ident :answer.type/text {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}] [:db/ident :course-academy.source/editor {:db/doc "User-directed content formatting edits in Course Academy." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502323 :db/ident :course-academy.source/editor}] [:course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" {:course/code "MTH 341" :course/description "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves." :course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "c21e5c95-26cd-5756-9092-0f79c13dc069" :course-outcome/index 12 :course-outcome/text "Explain, at an introductory level, how eigenvalue methods can be used in solving simple systems of linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406537} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d4780100-cd7f-58a2-8145-face3fff2460" :course-outcome/index 11 :course-outcome/text "Relate algebraic and geometric multiplicities to the availability of an eigenvector basis and diagonalize a matrix when such a basis exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406538} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02fb05-51ae-5983-9ed2-7e2763532ea8" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Calculate eigenvalues and eigenvectors, including examples involving nonreal complex values, and determine the associated eigenspaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406539} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "15723e90-f0cc-5ee0-9f2e-05c5f5aed323" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Use Gaussian elimination to solve linear systems and describe their complete solution sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406540} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "26f3092c-2231-5b66-80de-d4a832531472" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Calculate determinants and use their properties to assess invertibility and the uniqueness of solutions to square linear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406541} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3ef6ac4e-6cad-594f-83a6-5f66d9769392" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Perform matrix operations and determine whether a square matrix has an inverse, calculating it when one exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406542} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a152871e-0fe3-5370-8e14-1c769972f5c4" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Interpret matrix transformations geometrically and relate composition of transformations to matrix multiplication." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406543} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0ab3c082-09e3-558c-8ee4-d04898b4949c" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Construct and use the standard-basis matrix representation of a linear transformation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406544} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8c76e2f7-3c8c-5569-b169-2cc27e8fa04d" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Apply the rank–nullity relationship to reason about solution sets and matrix invertibility." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406545} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d28777c9-3775-500a-8e78-8f1ec68fe02a" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Analyze linear dependence and independence and determine the span of a collection of vectors." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406546} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "35e7d779-26db-56d0-9c90-8d096e4d6754" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Find bases and dimensions for row, column, and null spaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406547} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "753ae7d6-d7b2-545e-b987-f70e367561c7" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Determine whether a subset of \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) satisfies the requirements for a subspace." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406548}] :course/overview "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Linear Algebra I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405206}] [:topi